WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:01.770
- [Reporter] Less than a year after

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:04.166
the US Supreme Court
overturned Roe v. Wade,

00:00:04.166 --> 00:00:05.910
(crowd cheers)

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.460
the issue of abortion
returned to the high court

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.610
where justices decided to
temporarily preserve access

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.223
to a drug used in most
medication abortions.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.850
Abortion access across
the US varies greatly

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.380
and continues to change.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.170
- What you have today
is you have a situation

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:25.020
that is much more state-specific,

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.030
and I would say much more volatile.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:27.863
- [Reporter] Here's a look

00:00:27.863 --> 00:00:29.700
at how the abortion landscape has changed

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.416
since Roe v. Wade was overturned.

00:00:31.416 --> 00:00:32.700
(crowd cheers)

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.640
Abortion access is currently
protected in 18 states

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.470
and the District of Columbia.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.170
12 states have near-total
bans on abortion.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.410
In 10 states, abortion is
neither banned nor protected.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.360
The remaining 10 states have laws

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.460
that significantly limit abortion,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.520
but not all of them are
currently being enforced.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.070
- Some of the most striking
changes you've seen

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.020
are in Republican-led states

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.720
where you've seen abortion go away

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.420
and some cases, almost overnight.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.253
- [Reporter] In Florida,

00:01:01.253 --> 00:01:03.870
legislators recently passed
a bill banning abortion

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.680
after six weeks of pregnancy
with limited exceptions.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.370
Governor Ron DeSantis signed
the bill into law April 13th,

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.580
but it isn't poised to go
into effect immediately.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.470
- Florida was one of the few places

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.660
in the South where
abortion was still legal.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.610
It saw one of the biggest increases

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.610
in abortions after the
overturning of Roe v. Wade.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.080
If this goes into effect,

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.750
this will have a significant effect

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.660
on abortion access in the
south and around the country.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.850
- At the same time, some
conservative leaning

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.600
or politically mixed states
have increased abortion access.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.420
In Michigan, voters approved
a constitutional amendment

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.850
enshrining protections
for abortion rights,

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.370
nullifying a pre-Roe ban.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:47.550
Governor Gretchen Whitmer signed
it into law in early April.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.950
- No one is gonna
restrict our reproductive

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.380
and our reproductive rights and freedom

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.850
in the state, no one.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:54.960
- [Reporter] As states hash out

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.520
their own abortion regulations,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.190
a legal battle has caught
national attention,

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:02.280
and prompted the supreme
court to intervene.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.020
The battle started in Texas

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.330
where a district judge
temporarily suspended

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.370
the food and drug
administration's approval

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:09.750
of the drug Mifepristone

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.240
which is used in most
medication abortions.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.930
- The decision would severely
harm women across the country.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.830
It would deprive patients of a
safe and effective medication

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.810
to manage their reproductive health.

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.220
- [Reporter] Days later, another ruling

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.810
from the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.790
blocked part of that Texas ruling,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.220
allowing Mifepristone
to remain on the market,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.500
but with some significant restrictions

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.480
including when the pill can be taken

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.970
and how it can be prescribed.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.790
The Biden administration
appealed the Texas decision

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.160
and on April 21st, the
supreme court decided

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.950
to temporarily preserve
access to Mifepristone

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.140
while litigation in
lower courts plays out,

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.650
which means, the FDA, drug
makers, and abortion providers

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.690
have more time before an official ruling

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.430
on Mifepristone is made.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.410
- It's actually the first time

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.740
we've seen abortion access
meaningfully in peril,

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.810
even in liberal states, even in places

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.020
with democratic governors
and democratic legislatures,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.140
because if the FDA were
forced to pull this drug

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.480
from the market, that
would affect everyone,

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.499
no matter where you live in the country.

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:15.472
(crowd chatter)
(gentle music)

